WEBVTT

00:00:00.102 --> 00:00:05.015
Missing text and blank text are not the same thing A
null

00:00:05.041 --> 00:00:09.540
value means the value is missing or unknown An empty
string means

00:00:09.566 --> 00:00:13.561
we have text but its length is zero We can also have

00:00:13.586 --> 00:00:19.476
text containing only spaces In real datasets all
three situations can appear

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:23.947
A useful pattern is to first trim spaces using B
trim and

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:28.839
then use null if to convert an empty result into
null The

00:00:28.865 --> 00:00:32.164
query on this slide finds email addresses that are
either missing or

00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:36.843
effectively blank Run it directly in the browser and
inspect the records

00:00:36.856 --> 00:00:41.328
it finds You can also copy the SQL to pgAdmin and
run

00:00:41.354 --> 00:00:45.722
it there This kind of normalization is very useful
before we start

00:00:45.748 --> 00:00:48.880
making decisions about whether a contact value is
usable
